  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 216 PM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nBroken to overcast stratocumulus clouds will continue for much\nof the afternoon as moist air returns on swly winds. This will\nkeep high temps in the middle 70s to lower 80s depending on\nlocation. Otherwise will be watching a very weak sfc trough and\nwind shift that will affect Upper MI and far nern WI late this\nafternoon and evening. It will be weakly supported aloft by a\nvorticity maximum moving across the region. MLCAPE of 1000 J/KG\nand effective shear of 40 kt is expected with some sunshine\npossible late this afternoon. However, will only forecast 20 \npercent chances for showers and storms given the weak convergence.\n\nWith relative humidity remaining high today and weak sfc \nridging expected late tnt, areas of fog and low stratus will be \npossible early Sunday morning especially over nern WI.\n\nFor Sunday a weak wave of low pressure will move ewd across nrn\nOntario and be supported aloft by a shortwave trough tracking\nacross the same region and toward srn Ontario late in the day.\nThus the better warm advection and PVA will be to the north with\nweaker warm, moist advection shifting ewd across ne and east\ncentral WI. Isolated to scattered storms (20-30 percent) will be\npossible on the leading edge of the mid level cap, mostly over\nfar ern WI. The warm advection and much more sunshine than today\nwill boost high temps into the upper 80s to lower 90s, with\ndewpoints into the lower 70s over central WI. Heat indices\nreaching 100F are looking marginal at this time for central WI,\nso no heat headlines, but will continue to message hot and \nhumid conditions.\n\nFor late Sun nt into Mon evening a shortwave trough will track\nfrom the Canadian Prairies to the nrn Great Lakes with a 1000 mb\nsfc low reaching far east central WI by 00Z Tue. Its swly low\nlevel jet should initiate convection Sun nt over nrn MN toward\nnw WI, with some storms moving into north central WI before 12Z\nMon. The showers and storms will then track sewd with new\ndevelopment expected throughout the day on Mon via low to mid \nlevel warm, moist advection and frontogenesis. The cold front\nwill follow the low and mostly pass through the area by early\nevening. MUCAPE values are challenging to determine given clouds\nand showers and storms to begin the day, but still have fairly\nhigh confidence in minimal amounts of MUCAPE around 1500 J/KG.  \nStrong deep layer shear of 50 kt will be present with veering\nwinds in the low to mid levels from swly to nwly. Thus all\nhazards including tornadoes are possible for Mon. The early\nmorning storms will also have a SVR wind and hail potential.\n\nAfterward, this low pressure area and upper wave will occlude\nand remain over portions of the Mid Atlantic States and New\nEngland for Wed and the remainder of the week. This maintains\nnwly flow aloft for the wrn Great Lakes with a sfc ridge\nresiding over the area for Tue nt-Thu. Shortwave troughs in the\nflow aloft will then bring 20-40 percent chances for showers and\nstorms Thu-Sat. High temps in the lower to middle 80s are\nexpected for much of this period.",
